As we go through the steps, I'll talk about different tools you can use in these different scenarios.

49
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:42,900
All right, so let's go through the 10 steps to the FMEA process.

50
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:47,980
Steps 1 and 2 are to essentially define your system or process to be analyzed.

51
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:53,060
You want to be clear around the boundaries that you're putting on your process or system.

52
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:59,740
I would recommend smaller is better if you try to analyze a really complex design or a really complex process.

53
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:05,660
It can feel like boiling the ocean, so step one is to define exactly the boundaries of what we're analyzing.

54
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:08,100
And then step two is to identify those failure modes.

55
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:10,660
This falls into the category of risk identification.

56
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:16,420
We're essentially identifying those failure modes that could impact our customer.

57
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:20,220
And then steps three through nine is all about risk analysis.

58
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:23,020
This is where we take the risks we've identified.

59
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:26,020
We talk about severity, occurrence and detection.

60
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:50,980
This is the risk analysis process and at the end of the day we're going to calculate an RPN number and then finally is corrective actions if we take our RPN number and we compare it against our acceptance criteria right what is the acceptable level of risk that we're willing to accept or not accept now we need to take corrective actions right let's say we have a really high RPN it's time to mitigate that risk reduce that risk and ultimately control that risk through corrective actions.

61
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:53,780
Alright, so let's get into the 10-step process here.

62
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:59,900
Step zero: this was not on the previous slide, but it is a really important step before you even start the PFMEA.

63
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:01,420
You have to establish the ground rules.

64
00:07:01,740 --> 00:07:08,940
Now I'm assuming that your organization has some type of FMEA template that you use, which has predefined scales.

65
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:16,620
But if you don't have that, one of the first things you have to do is define the scales that you plan on using for severity, occurrence, and detection.

66
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:28,640
And remember, this is unique to your product, this is unique to your industry and your customer because especially the severity scores they can very much differ depending on the types of products you're making.

67
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:31,840
Other things you should do before you ever start is document any assumptions.

68
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:39,680
Your FMEA is a living document so three five ten years down the road somebody's going to read this and you want to make sure you've clearly communicated your assumptions.

69
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:47,200
For example one of the most important starting assumptions in FMEA is that your raw materials are all in specification.

70
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:51,940
That's a pretty common assumption, and you just want to document whatever those assumptions are.

71
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:57,140
And then the last thing you have to do, and this is super important, is defining your acceptable risk level.

72
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:11,780
Every organization has a different appetite for risk, your customers have a certain appetite for risk, and so you have to in your risk policy define what you believe is an acceptable and unacceptable level of risk for your product or for your organization.

73
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:25,360
That's important to do upfront before you ever get started because if you wait until the end to define your acceptable level of risk, there's a chance that some bias might creep into the process and you might end up accepting more risk than you otherwise would have.

74
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:29,160
By the way, these scales are super arbitrary; I'm showing one through six.

75
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:33,400
Some organizations do one through ten, and I've seen one, three, five, seven, nine.

76
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:39,400
You can do this any way you want to, but just define these upfront and use them consistently throughout the process.

77
00:08:40,159 --> 00:08:42,760
Step number one, define your system.

78
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:49,240
Remember, we want to scope our FMEA—whether it's a PFMEA or a DFMEA—we want to scope that upfront.

79
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:53,360
If you're doing a PFMEA, one of the best ways to do this is with a flow diagram.

80
00:08:53,920 --> 00:09:00,280
You can take this visual representation of your process and use it to establish the boundaries of your process.

81
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:05,840
You can also use this to identify all of the key manufacturing steps within your process.

82
00:09:06,340 --> 00:09:16,980
If you're doing a DFMEA, you can use something like a system block diagram to visually show all of the interfaces and relationships between the different components and subsystems in your design.

83
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:18,420
So you can see it here, right?

84
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:23,420
We've got our components, we've got maybe a subsystem, and then we've got the entire system of our product.

85
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:31,220
Again, I recommend you try to narrow the scope as tightly as possible just to make the process a little easier and a little simpler.

86
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:35,860
If you blow out the scope, people can get burnt out, and then the process fails.

87
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,240
Again, if you can try to limit the scope as best as possible.

88
00:09:39,840 --> 00:09:44,600
And then step number two, once you've identified the scope, it's time to talk about potential failure modes.

89
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:46,800
So what is a failure mode?

90
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:54,160
Well, a failure is any nonconformance or error, or you could call it a defect associated with your process.

91
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:57,120
Now what we're looking for are failure modes.

92
00:09:57,480 --> 00:10:03,560
So a failure mode, I use the word mode, is the mode, method, or way in which something might fail.

93
00:10:03,620 --> 00:10:06,860
Essentially, what we're asking here is: what might go wrong?

94
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:10,700
How are those different ways that our process might fail?

95
00:10:10,780 --> 00:10:15,060
What could go wrong that we would end up creating a nonconformance or a defect?

96
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:17,980
So, that's a failure mode for a DFMEA.

97
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:21,820
What I recommend is that people start with their design drawings.

98
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:24,700
You've got this new design; you've got components and subsystems.

99
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:32,180
What you want to look for are features that, if they were out of specification, would directly impact the functionality of your product.

100
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:34,540
This is a critical-to-quality attribute.

101
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:38,100
And this can help you when you're tolerancing and dimensioning your product.

102
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:44,900
If you have a product feature that is directly related to functionality, maybe you need really tight tolerances.

103
00:10:45,140 --> 00:10:54,220
But if you have other features that are maybe only indirectly related to functionality or they're not related to functionality at all, maybe you can widen those tolerances a little bit.

104
00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:55,820
By the way, I love this picture over here on the right.

105
00:10:56,060 --> 00:11:01,520
I want to emphasize that the entire FMEA process, whether design or process, It is a team sport.

106
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:17,160
You need to make sure you've got the right subject matter experts from manufacturing engineering to process engineering, to product engineering, R&D, reliability, quality engineering, all the different functions and subject matter experts should get together to participate in the FMEA process.

107
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:19,880
And then over here, I've got this picture of this destructive test.

108
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:27,480
You need to use prototype testing and reliability testing and all sorts of data to feed into your design FMEA.

109
00:11:28,060 --> 00:11:33,300
You're going to make a lot of assumptions early in this DFMEA, and you want to back that up with real data and real testing.

110
00:11:33,900 --> 00:11:43,940
So over time throughout the design process, as you collect data about the ways in which your product fails, feed that back into your DFMEA and refine those estimates of risk.

111
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:54,780
And then for the PFMEA, if you're doing a process FMEA and you're wondering about how your process might fail, the most important thing you can do is walk your process.

112
00:11:55,500 --> 00:12:02,860
Take your subject matter experts, go down to the floor, watch the process in action and see how your process might fail.

113
00:12:03,260 --> 00:12:08,100
Talk to the operators, talk to the supervisor and understand what goes wrong on a daily basis.

114
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:18,700
The other thing that's important to note here is that not only do you want to document actual failures that have occurred, you want to do a little bit of brainstorming to talk about potential failure modes.

115
00:12:18,740 --> 00:12:25,140
This is a really powerful element of the FMEA process where we don't just talk about the actual risks that have occurred.

116
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:31,620
We identify potential risks and then we can mitigate those things and prevent them from ever occurring in the first place.

117
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:38,300
And I've got this picture over here for data, it's really important again to use whatever data you have to drive the process.

118
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:53,060
If you have other similar processes that have known failure modes, use those, take that and inject it into your new PFMEA and use that data around the occurrence rate or the detectability and to better estimate things like severity, occurrence and detection.

119
00:12:54,180 --> 00:13:01,980
And then the effects, so when we talk about the effect of a failure mode, what we're really talking about is the impact on the end user.

120
00:13:02,420 --> 00:13:07,900
When our process fails, when this failure mode occurs, what is the effect on the end user?

121
00:13:07,940 --> 00:13:13,180
And we want to have that discussion because your effect is what drives your severity score.

122
00:13:13,540 --> 00:13:20,580
So here we are, we're down here where we're talking about the effects of the failure, and there's a reason that we put effects right next to severity.

123
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:26,660
The impact on the end user should drive your severity score, and I want to show you this graphic.

124
00:13:26,700 --> 00:13:28,820
We have a failure mode, right?

125
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:31,860
And that results in some impact to the end user.

126
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:36,740
Now, in a minute, we're going to talk about the root causes because what we want to address is the root cause.

127
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:44,460
But before we do that, we have to talk about how these failure modes impact the end user because again, that's what drives severity.

128
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:55,640
Now, the way we talk about severity is essentially a measure of the degree to which the end user is impacted or affected by the failure mode.

129
00:13:55,840 --> 00:14:03,440
Again, I'm showing this severity score as a 1 through 6 scale; there are also 1 through 10 scales and various other scales.

130
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:12,520
However, you want to define this scale in relation to your own product and the severity of your specific unique failure modes.

131
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:37,320
That are related back to your product, but again I'm showing one through six, six being the worst right so if you have a severity score of a six here that is a catastrophic impact to the end user, the product is inoperable it results in an unsafe working condition and could result in harm to the end user, a score of a one is something like no relevant effect on reliability or safety or functionality that's bottom of the barrel.

132
00:14:37,780 --> 00:14:39,700
And then now we can talk about causes.

133
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:45,980
Remember, we've identified failure modes at this point, we've analyzed the effect, and now it's time to talk about root causes.

134
00:14:46,660 --> 00:14:50,460
And some of the best tools that you can use here are things like the five why analysis.

135
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:51,580
Why did that happen?

136
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:52,420
Why did that happen?

137
00:14:52,780 --> 00:14:55,700
To really drill down to the true root cause.

138
00:14:55,740 --> 00:15:00,020
And then again, it's a lot of brainstorming, especially when we're talking about potential failure modes.

139
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:03,020
The other tool that's really important to use here.

140
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:12,840
The fishbone diagram, the cause and effect diagram has a great mentality around identifying potential root causes specifically the eight Ms.

141
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:32,920
So if you've ever used a fishbone diagram you'll know that there's eight common categories of root causes man machine method materials mother nature measurement management and maintenance now I put an asterisk here next to materials because that's one of the starting assumptions in the FMEA process that our raw materials are all conforming to the specification.

142
00:15:33,340 --> 00:15:44,620
So this might be the one exception, but as you're talking about root causes, it's important to be holistic and to think broadly because if you don't identify the right root cause, you can't eliminate that cause, right?

143
00:15:44,700 --> 00:15:49,220
You can't reduce the risk or you can't mitigate the risk if you haven't really identified the root cause.

144
00:15:49,740 --> 00:15:52,820
So this is a really important step in the process.

145
00:15:52,860 --> 00:15:59,180
And then once we understand those root causes, we can use those causes to estimate the occurrence.

146
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:26,740
So the occurrence ranking, this you can see it in blue down here, this score is again I'm showing as a one to six scale is a reflection of the likelihood or the probability that a failure will occur if you don't have any data you can simply do this using an expert opinion on a semi quantitative scale right one through six if you understand things like process capability or defects per million or the probability of a failure.

147
00:16:26,820 --> 00:16:36,940
Those are other more quantitative ways to estimate probability, if you have the data, you should use it here because it makes your estimate of risk that much more accurate.

148
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:39,200
So what is a control?

149
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:45,800
A control is anything in your process that might prevent or detect the failure mode being analyzed.

150
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:48,080
Now, what I'm showing here is a control plan.

151
00:16:48,680 --> 00:16:53,240
There is a very tight relationship between your PFMEA and your control plan.

152
00:16:53,880 --> 00:17:00,240
We create a control plan to monitor and control our process in an effort to mitigate and reduce risk.

153
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:05,119
We create a PFMEA to identify risks that should be controlled and monitored in our process.

154
00:17:05,579 --> 00:17:12,020
These two documents should be created hand in hand because you want to control the risks that you are aware of.

155
00:17:12,579 --> 00:17:19,940
So your control plan and your PFMEA are very much related, and again, it is just your ability to prevent or detect failures.

156
00:17:20,020 --> 00:17:24,500
Once you've defined your controls in your process, it's time to talk about detection.

157
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:29,460
So your detection is a reflection of the capability of your process.

158
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:34,480
To identify a failure mode once it has occurred, that's a really important point by the way.

159
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,480
Remember the detection score is based on the assumption that the failure mode has occurred.

160
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:49,840
So no matter what the frequency of occurrence is, let's assume it has occurred and now let's talk about how likely we are to actually detect that failure mode once it has occurred.

161
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,480
And again, this comes back to our control plan, right?

162
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:57,160
How well does our control plan detect that failure mode once it has occurred?

163
00:17:57,220 --> 00:17:58,420
And then let's give it a score.

164
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:07,140
We can talk about it in qualitative terms, like we're certain to catch it, where we have a high probability or low probability, or maybe it's undetectable.

165
00:18:07,300 --> 00:18:13,900
Then you can put it in more semi-quantitative terms: we have 50% detection or 75% detection.

166
00:18:14,540 --> 00:18:18,340
Those are different ways to score detectability.

167
00:18:18,740 --> 00:18:28,660
Now remember, when you improve detectability, your ranking goes from like a six to a five to a four because you're essentially getting better at detecting that failure mode.

168
00:18:28,700 --> 00:18:32,620
And again, that's the last assessment we make on a failure mode.

169
00:18:32,860 --> 00:18:34,740
Now we're ready to calculate RPN.

170
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:36,500
So what is RPN?

171
00:18:36,540 --> 00:18:43,420
RPN stands for Risk Priority Number, and it's calculated as severity times occurrence times detection.

172
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:49,380
Let's say we have this example: we have a failure mode with a severity of 4, an occurrence of 6, and a detection of 5.

173
00:18:50,100 --> 00:18:54,380
Well, our RPN number is simply 4 times 6 times 5, which equals 120.

174
00:18:55,120 --> 00:19:18,880
The reason that we like RPN and the reason that RPN is so valuable is it gives you an objective prioritization tool when you go through the FMEA process, you're going to identify hundreds if not thousands of failure modes and you cannot work on all of them at the same time, you need a tool to help you prioritize and focus your time on the highest risk or the highest priority failure modes in terms of corrective actions.

